<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Litani River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Litani is the longest river wholly within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, about 140 kilometres long. It rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa Valley</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a>, flows south between the Lebanon and Anti-Lebanon ranges, then turns west through a deep gorge below Beaufort Castle to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>. It is an important source of water and hydroelectric power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/litani-river.jpg" alt="Litani River" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sonia Rachid (Sonia Fares) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Litani_river_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
